resonance index j = 6d(2d+1) and the reading "the only resonant index in the whole ray is the t = 2
negative fibre" are new but small. Not a mechanism for the all-t proof.

### 1.2 What structurally forces it — the honest answer, and the new lever

Nothing LOCAL forces it (Astra: formal solutions at x = 0 with Bη ≠ 0 exist for every t and merely fail
to truncate). Nothing at the TOP forces it (the leading balance is an identity on the ray). Nothing
DEGREE-ONLY forces it (17(ppppp) DEGREE-BLIND). So the forcing must be a GLOBAL identity among the
2t−1 generators. Two observations narrow what that identity can look like.

(a) **It is an exact square, not a radical, at every banked index.** Astra records the direct exact
exponent 2 at t = 3, 4 (τ² ∈ I_+, τ ∉ I_+). If τ_t² ∈ I_{t,+} for all t, the statement is a
Nullstellensatz CERTIFICATE τ_t² = Σ_k a_k T_{t,k} with a_k weighted-homogeneous of weight
2(4t+1) − wt(T_{t,k}). Certificates of this kind on the ray have so far always had closed forms in t
(the spine substitutions, RESIDUAL-ZERO, TOP-TAIL-UNIT). The mechanism I propose is therefore
"prove the SQUARE identity uniformly from its cofactor structure", and the first thing to learn is
the SUPPORT of the cofactors: if only the tail rows T_{t,t..2t−1} appear, the identity lives in the
tail, whose structure is already uniform (TOP-TAIL-UNIT); if the low rows appear, the identity is a
genuinely full-ideal statement and the tail route is dead. Either outcome is decisive for how Astra's
lane should spend its time.

(b) **The geometric meaning fixes what τ ≠ 0 would be.** From the banked identity
J_{γ,π}(Q,P) = −cz + τπ (z = π − γ, c = −yg): a cone point is a polynomial pair whose Jacobian is a
NONZERO LINEAR FORM, and (8.1) says that form is rigidly proportional to z. Moh's Prop 6.3(3), read
from the page image (p.197), says the descent of a Keller pair has Jacobian EXACTLY −(u_s/b)·γ^ℓ in
the descent variable γ (the ray is u_s = 1, d_s = 4, v_s = 3, so ℓ = 1 — a linear form on the nose).
So a τ ≠ 0 point is a "tilted-critical-line" pair: same shape, same Newton polygon, Jacobian a
linear form whose zero line is not the descent axis. This is the k = 1 monomial-Jacobian receiver of
row 1 with a rotated critical line. It gives a second, structural route: show that for pairs of the
ray's Newton shape (monic in π, deg_γ[π^k] ≤ 3k), a linear Jacobian must vanish on the γ-axis. I do
not have that theorem; §6 Card A gives the cheapest test of whether it is even true at the one place
where an explicit family exists (t = 2, y = 1/5).

### 1.3 Cheapest decisive test (bounded)

- **Test Q1-A (certificate structure).** In Singular, on the banked exact terminal ideals at t = 3, 4
  (box/k16astra-20260905 has the arrays; the ideal is 2t−1 generators in t variables): compute
  `lift(I_+, tau^2)` and record (i) success (exponent 2 confirmed), (ii) the set of k with a_k ≠ 0,
  (iii) whether the a_k depend on b3. Repeat at t = 5 (needs the t = 5 chart; 17(qqq) has mod-1009
  data on H₅, so exact-Q may need one generation). Bounded quantities: N_t = min{N : τ^N ∈ I_+} for
  t = 3,4,5 (prediction: 2) and |supp(a)|. Wall clock: minutes at t = 3, 4; ≤ 30 min at t = 5.
  Interpretation: tail-supported ⇒ the uniform proof is a tail identity (Astra should target it);
  full-supported with N = 2 ⇒ aim at a uniform square identity through the Abel closing polynomials;
  N growing with t ⇒ the exact-square lever is dead, only radical/valuative arguments remain.
- **Test Q1-B (falsification, standing).** t = 11 is the first split index after t = 2 with both
  fibres rational (d = ±2, y = 5/23 and 7/23). The resonance index there is 36 (d = −2), well outside
  the range 24, so the negative fibre has no pivot degeneracy; the only remaining reason to fear
  t = 11 is the non-Galois-protected fibre pair. Cheapest instrument that has NOT timed out: the
  weighted Hilbert function of I_{11,+} mod p by Macaulay linear algebra in weights ≤ 40 (dim-0 cone
  ⇒ H(w) = 0 for large w, promotable by properness since the ideal is homogeneous); bounded
  quantity: the first weight with H(w) = 0, or a positive plateau (positive-dimensional cone, then
  (8.1) needs the finer test). Cost: minutes per weight up to w ≈ 40. I did not run it here.

### 1.4 What I would tell Astra's running lane (receipt only)

The coefficient-sensitive solution theorem is the right target but "generic Abel counts do not supply
it" is only half the diagnosis: the linked data (A, D, ω) are all TOP-weight data, and §1.1 shows the
top determines everything down to weight 2t — the theorem must be about the last three weights
(2t, 2t+1, 2t+2), where the ODE degenerates to the quadratic top row in b. So the theorem is: "the two
polynomials η(c,b), B(c,b) cannot both be nonzero on the double cover of V(F)". Formulate it in those
terms, and use Test Q1-A's cofactor support to choose between a tail identity and a cover argument.

