### 2.2 The descents (Prop 6.3, read from the page image)

Moh p.197 (PDF 58), Prop 6.3: with θ = y⁻¹, z = y − bx − e, the π-root is σ = Σ a_j θ^j + π·θ^{v_s/u_s}
and γ = θ^{1/u_s}; the descended elements ḡ(σ), T̄_i(σ) lie in k[γ,π], are monic in π of π-degrees
u_s·n/d_s and u_s·(−μ_i)/d_s, and (3) J_{γ,π}(ḡ(σ), T̄₁(σ)) = −(u_s/b)·γ^{v_s−u_s−1}. So the descent is
the explicit substitution y = γ^{−u_s}, x = (y − e − σ)/b, whose own Jacobian is that monomial; the
descended Jacobian is a monomial in γ (the y^{−1/u_s} variable), NOT in π. (I first wrote a
"π-degree screen" ℓ ≤ n′+m′−1 for these rows; it is vacuous because J has π-degree 0, and I withdrew
it before use — a FALLACY-v2 variable/ring-map catch, recorded in memory.) Cor 6.1 (p.199) is the
case d_s = 3, ℓ = 0: a genuine smaller Keller pair — Moh's induction step.

Applying u_s = d_s − V_s, n′ = n·u_s/d_s, ℓ = v_s − u_s − 1 = d_s − 3, M′_i = M_i·u_s/d_s:

| rows | d_s | (n′, m′; ℓ) | M′ | V′ |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| (90,60)×4 | 5 | (18,12; 2) | (2,9) or (9,16) | (1,8),(3,8),(2,5),(3,5) |
| (96,64)×4 | 4 | (24,16; 1) | (12,17); s=5 row (−12,−2,5) | (1,2),(2,1),(3,2); (1,1,6) |
| (96,72)×3 | 4 | (24,18; 1) | (−15,14) or (9,20) | (1,9)×2,(4,9) |
| (96,72)×3 | 6 | (16,12; 3) | (6,13) | (1,1),(1,3),(4,3) |

Four descended degree data, 13 distinct (n′,m′,ℓ,V′) classes for 14 rows. Class-batching saves one
chart. The descended pairs are small (π-degrees 16–24), ℓ ∈ {1,2,3}, and — the structural point —
their depth is s′ = s−1 ∈ {3,4}: every one of the 14 carries at least two intermediate characteristic
levels after descent.

### 2.3 Is there a uniform argument across the 14? Not a numerical one; a structural one exists in
statement but not in proof

Uniformities that are TRUE: all 14 are u_s = 1 (radius automatic, Prop 6.4); the descent is one
substitution; ℓ = d_s − 3 ∈ {1,2,3}; polynomiality of g in y forces the Newton bound
deg_γ[π^k] ḡ ≤ v_s·k on the descended pair (from π = y^{v_s}(z − Σ a_j y^{−j}), coefficient by
coefficient in z). Uniformities that are FALSE: the descended ratio (2/3 for (90,60),(96,64); 3/4 for
(96,72)); the depth pattern (M′ sign patterns differ); V′. So there is no single (K,ℓ)-lattice chart
covering them, and Moh's own printed table (Appendix II, p.207: columns n, m, M₂, V₂, δ₂, δ₁, J) is an
s′ = 2 table — the 14 are exactly the shape his worked method never displays (consistent with their
being an "artifact" of his private program rather than a printed screen).

The one structural lever I see: 7 of the 14 have ℓ = 1, so their descended pairs have a LINEAR
Jacobian c·γ — the k = 1 receiver of row 1 — and the same is true of the whole K16 ray (§4.2). A
theorem "a pair of Newton shape deg_γ[π^k] ≤ 3k with Jacobian c·γ is fold∘automorphism" would kill
those 7 and the K16 atom together; it is a JC2-strength statement in general (a Keller map composed
with the fold (γ²/2, π) has Jacobian γ), but the SHAPE restriction may make it provable. §6 Card A
prices its cheapest test. For the ℓ = 2, 3 rows (7 of 14) the order chart is the only instrument.

