### 3.2 The lattice, corrected

The right index set is (e, d; K′; ℓ; s′; M′, V′): the reduced degree ratio (e,d) is a coordinate
(the (96,72) rows descend to ratio 4/3, the (90,60) rows to 3/2); ℓ = v_s − u_s − 1 is the difficulty
axis (ℓ = 0 is Moh's Cor 6.1 — a smaller Keller pair, i.e. the induction; ℓ = 1 is the linear-
Jacobian receiver; ℓ ≥ 2 is the general monomial receiver); s′ is the depth. The k = 4 ray is the
line (e,d) = (3,2), ℓ = 4, s′ = 2; the K16 ray is (e,d) = (3t+1, 2t+1), ℓ = 1, s′ = 2 (§4.2). Both
flagship rays are u_s = 1 rays (their admissible ancestors are u_s = 1: 17(gggggg) for K = 7,8,9;
(64,48) for K16), so "u_s ≥ 2 census ∧ u_s = 1 rays" is not the split; the split is s′ = 2 (rays,
charts exist) versus s′ ≥ 3 (97% of the residue, no chart exists).

### 3.3 The path, priced

1. **Route-to-state at s′ ≥ 3 (OPEN[DESCENT-STATE-S3], 17(jj)) is the binding routing map.** Every
   other map (first-separation, ordinary-support, receiver coverage) is exercised inside a chart; no
   chart exists for the state 97% of the residue lands in. Cheapest test: build the s′ = 3 chart for
