   K16 atom at once. Its truth is testable at t = 2 (Card A).

## 4. Q4 — the finish: challenging (H1)∧(H2)∧(routing); the missing case; the single next object

### 4.1 The framing, corrected in three places

- **"Census sweep ∧ K16-type rays" treats u_s ≥ 2 as the sweep and u_s = 1 as automatic.** Measured,
  u_s = 1 is 78% of the residue and BOTH flagship rays are u_s = 1. The (H2) "descent" half is not the
  small half. The framing's "descended-class sweep" also inherits a compression figure that does not
  apply at the datum the chart needs (§3.1(iii)).
- **The missing case is depth, not a configuration.** The 12:00Z synthesis listed "s′ > 2 residual"
  as a leak; it is 97% of the residue and includes all 14 rows of the ≤ 100 theorem and 8 of the 9
  D = 108 survivors. Until an s′ ≥ 3 chart exists the sweep cannot start, and the running 14-row lane
  cannot finish as charged (§2.4).
- **"D = 108 CLOSED at skeleton level" and "(99,66) closed degree-wide" are not parallel statements.**
  The (99,66) closure is degree-wide because the operative screen leaves only S8 there; at D = 108
  the screen leaves nine rows and only the printed one is dead. The packet should carry the
  degree-wide survivor count next to every "closed" line (§8 upgrade).

### 4.2 The K16 ray is the ℓ = 1 receiver's first ray (new connection: K16 ↔ (H1) u_s = 1 rows ↔ row 1)

Banked facts assembled: the ray passes through Moh's (64,48) at t = 1 (17(v)); its descended charts
in `order_basis_full.py` are (16,12; 13; 3; k=1) and (28,20; 25; 3; k=1), i.e. (n′,m′) = (4(3t+1),
4(2t+1)) = (4e, 4q) at t = 1, 2; hence the ray is (n,m) = (16(3t+1), 16(2t+1)) with d_s = 4, V_s = 3,
u_s = 1, v_s = 3, ℓ = 1 — check at (64,48): d = (64,16,4,…), V₃ = 3, u_s = 1 ✓. So a K16 cone point is
a pair in k[γ,π] of π-degrees (4e,4q), Newton shape deg_γ[π^k] ≤ 3k, Jacobian a linear form; (8.1)
says the form is c·γ. The 671 ℓ = 1 rows at D ≤ 200 (and 7 of the 14, 2 of the D = 108 eight) are the
same object at other (e,d) and depths. A theorem about the ℓ = 1 receiver is therefore the only
statement in the campaign that is uniform across BOTH halves of (H1)∧(H2). Row 29's language fits
it exactly: for J(f,g) = γ the Hamiltonian derivation D_f has D_f(g) = −γ, a "slice with a coordinate
value" instead of a slice.

### 4.3 Is the two-point stratum genuinely reducible to (sweep) ∧ (rays) ∧ (routing)? Yes, with the
depth case added and one standing scope item

With s′ ≥ 3 added to "routing" (as route-to-state) and u_s = 1 rays added to "rays", I see no further
missing configuration inside the two-point M_s = n−2 stratum: split/no-split is exhausted per row by
the split-face screen (17(iiiiii) made the survivor list a theorem), the radius is discharged by
complement (17(gggggg)), U-NEGATIVE is a per-row chart condition. Outside the stratum, the standing
item is my OPEN[PROP-5.4-MINIMALITY] (0000Z §4.1): if Moh's p.183 "Moreover" clause holds as printed,
a minimal-total-degree counterexample IS two-point, and the program is plane JC2 for minimal
counterexamples; it is a one-lane-hour source read that the packet still does not list as attempted.
I re-state it as the cheapest scope decision available and do not re-raise it.

### 4.4 The single most valuable next object

> **The ℓ = 1 receiver theorem**: for polynomial pairs (f,g) ∈ k[γ,π]² monic in π with
> deg_γ[π^k] ≤ 3k and J(f,g) = c·γ, decide whether the fold involution is polynomial (equivalently,
> whether (f,g) = H∘(γ²/2, π)∘G with J(H) = 1 and G ∈ Aut, so that the pair is Keller-composed-with-
> fold and its shape is then classifiable).

Why this and not the builder: the builder (Card B) is the critical-path INSTRUMENT and I make it the
first lane; but it is a finite, mechanizable object. The receiver theorem is the only candidate for
a statement that closes the K16 atom and 671 rows uniformly, and it has an explicit test bed — the
t = 2, y = 1/5 positive-dimensional cone, where actual pairs with J = −cz exist and can be checked for
fold type in an hour (Card A). If those pairs are fold-trivial, the (8.1) atom is "rigidity of the
fold direction" and the structural lane has a geometric target; if one is not, the ℓ = 1 receiver has
JC2-strength content and no uniform kill of the ℓ = 1 rows should be expected — which is also
decisive for how to spend the sweep.

### 4.5 Counterexample side, stated once

The honest positive signals now are: (i) a POSDIM verdict with J = const on an ℓ = 1 descended row
of the 14 or of the D = 108 eight (the smallest unvisited receiver slots below Moh's bound); (ii) a
non-fold linear-Jacobian pair at t = 2 (Card A) — not a counterexample, but the first sign that the
receiver is rich; (iii) a τ ≠ 0 point on the t = 11 negative fibre. A numerical lift is
REPRESENTATIVE, never a map.

